{Context:} Source-linked documentation can preserve provenance while misrepresenting linked evidence in reader-facing fields.
\textbf{Objective:} We evaluate four candidate explanation-fidelity dimensions and their released checker rules in a single LEAP case study, retaining only dimensions supported by independent reviewer evidence.
\textbf{Method:} Deterministic rules audit 28 frozen LEAP dossiers. Three independent reviewers evaluate the same blinded dossiers in unaided and guided phases. We measure agreement, compare checker output with majority labels, and analyze condition disclosure under two observed criteria.
\textbf{Results:} Source-role accuracy has Fleiss' $\kappa=0.407$, but the checker detects 2 of 16 majority failures (12.5\% recall). Condition disclosure has $\kappa=0.553$. Under the source-visibility criterion represented by R1/R2, condition-checker precision is 40\% with 100\% recall; under the reader-facing criterion represented by R3, the same eligible set yields 100\% precision and recall, a self-consistency result for the criterion the checker implements. Broad disagreement loses support under explicit-only sensitivity ($\kappa=-0.167$) and is narrowed to LEAP-specific explicit-conflict routing. Review-authority consistency fails reliability ($\kappa=-0.285$) across 84/84 explicit labels. Unaided majority identifies stable issues in 18/28 dossiers; five guided-only cases arise from the rejected authority candidate and are treated as order/priming artifacts.
\textbf{Conclusion:} Independent evaluation retains source-role accuracy and condition disclosure as publication dimensions while rejecting the current source-role automation as inadequate and showing condition-rule performance to be criterion-dependent. The study empirically separates construct support, criterion definition, and checker behavior within LEAP.
